A curse on their anger, for it is fierce;
a curse on their wrath, for it is cruel.
I will scatter them among the descendants of Jacob;
I will disperse them throughout Israel.

“Zebulun will settle by the seashore
and will be a harbor for ships;
his borders will extend to Sidon.

The leaders of Edom are terrified;
the nobles of Moab tremble.
All who live in Canaan melt away;

No misfortune is in his plan for Jacob;
no trouble is in store for Israel.
For the Lord their God is with them;
he has been proclaimed their king.

Water will flow from their buckets;
their offspring have all they need.
Their king will be greater than Agag;
their kingdom will be exalted.

Then he looked over toward the Kenites and delivered this message:
“Your home is secure;
your nest is set in the rocks.

Ships will come from the coasts of Cyprus;
they will oppress Assyria and afflict Eber,
but they, too, will be utterly destroyed.”

“But Israel soon became fat and unruly;
the people grew heavy, plump, and stuffed!
Then they abandoned the God who had made them;
they made light of the Rock of their salvation.

They have roused my jealousy by worshiping things that are not God;
they have provoked my anger with their useless idols.
Now I will rouse their jealousy through people who are not even a people;
I will provoke their anger through the foolish Gentiles.

Look now; I myself am he!
There is no other god but me!
I am the one who kills and gives life;
I am the one who wounds and heals;
no one can be rescued from my powerful hand!

Moses said this about the tribe of Asher:
“May Asher be blessed above other sons;
may he be esteemed by his brothers;
may he bathe his feet in olive oil.

They came down from Ephraim—
a land that once belonged to the Amalekites;
they followed you, Benjamin, with your troops.
From Makir the commanders marched down;
from Zebulun came those who carry a commander’s staff.

So he said:
“Out of the one who eats came something to eat;
out of the strong came something sweet.” Three days later they were still trying to figure it out.

No one is holy like the Lord!
There is no one besides you;
there is no Rock like our God.

Those who fight against the Lord will be shattered.
He thunders against them from heaven;
the Lord judges throughout the earth.
He gives power to his king;
he increases the strength of his anointed one.”

O mountains of Gilboa,
let there be no dew or rain upon you,
nor fruitful fields producing offerings of grain.
For there the shield of the mighty heroes was defiled;
the shield of Saul will no longer be anointed with oil.

O women of Israel, weep for Saul,
for he dressed you in luxurious scarlet clothing,
in garments decorated with gold.

But in my distress I cried out to the Lord;
yes, I cried to my God for help.
He heard me from his sanctuary;
my cry reached his ears.

“Then the earth quaked and trembled.
The foundations of the heavens shook;
they quaked because of his anger.

Smoke poured from his nostrils;
fierce flames leaped from his mouth.
Glowing coals blazed forth from him.

I consumed them;
I struck them down so they did not get up;
they fell beneath my feet.

“You gave me victory over my accusers.
You preserved me as the ruler over nations;
people I don’t even know now serve me.

and delivers me from my enemies.
You hold me safe beyond the reach of my enemies;
you save me from violent opponents.

You give great victories to your king;
you show unfailing love to your anointed,
to David and all his descendants forever.”

After consulting the people, the king appointed singers to walk ahead of the army, singing to the Lord and praising him for his holy splendor. This is what they sang:
“Give thanks to the Lord;
his faithful love endures forever!”

Let its morning stars remain dark.
Let it hope for light, but in vain;
may it never see the morning light.

If I am guilty, too bad for me;
and even if I’m innocent, I can’t hold my head high,
because I am filled with shame and misery.

The murderer rises in the early dawn
to kill the poor and needy;
at night he is a thief.

And this is what he says to all humanity:
‘The fear of the Lord is true wisdom;
to forsake evil is real understanding.’”

In a moment they die.
In the middle of the night they pass away;
the mighty are removed without human hand.

For you have added rebellion to your sin;
you show no respect,
and you speak many angry words against God.”

We cannot imagine the power of the Almighty;
but even though he is just and righteous,
he does not destroy us.

As the light approaches,
the earth takes shape like clay pressed beneath a seal;
it is robed in brilliant colors.

If a person does not repent,
God will sharpen his sword;
he will bend and string his bow.

The wicked conceive evil;
they are pregnant with trouble
and give birth to lies.

But in my distress I cried out to the Lord;
yes, I prayed to my God for help.
He heard me from his sanctuary;
my cry to him reached his ears.

Then the earth quaked and trembled.
The foundations of the mountains shook;
they quaked because of his anger.

Smoke poured from his nostrils;
fierce flames leaped from his mouth.
Glowing coals blazed forth from him.

The Lord thundered from heaven;
the voice of the Most High resounded
amid the hail and burning coals.

You have given me your shield of victory.
Your right hand supports me;
your help has made me great.

You gave me victory over my accusers.
You appointed me ruler over nations;
people I don’t even know now serve me.

and rescues me from my enemies.
You hold me safe beyond the reach of my enemies;
you save me from violent opponents.

You give great victories to your king;
you show unfailing love to your anointed,
to David and all his descendants forever.

Do not stay so far from me,
for trouble is near,
and no one else can help me.

My enemies surround me like a pack of dogs;
an evil gang closes in on me.
They have pierced my hands and feet.

Who is the King of glory?
The Lord, strong and mighty;
the Lord, invincible in battle.

Declare me innocent, O Lord,
for I have acted with integrity;
I have trusted in the Lord without wavering.

For he will conceal me there when troubles come;
he will hide me in his sanctuary.
He will place me out of reach on a high rock.

Do not let me fall into their hands.
For they accuse me of things I’ve never done;
with every breath they threaten me with violence.

O Lord, I have come to you for protection;
don’t let me be disgraced.
Save me, for you do what is right.

I will be glad and rejoice in your unfailing love,
for you have seen my troubles,
and you care about the anguish of my soul.

I am dying from grief;
my years are shortened by sadness.
Sin has drained my strength;
I am wasting away from within.

For you are my hiding place;
you protect me from trouble.
You surround me with songs of victory. Interlude

But they are glad now that I am in trouble;
they gleefully join together against me.
I am attacked by people I don’t even know;
they slander me constantly.

You have made my life no longer than the width of my hand.
My entire lifetime is just a moment to you;
at best, each of us is but a breath.” Interlude

Day and night I have only tears for food,
while my enemies continually taunt me, saying,
“Where is this God of yours?”

He causes wars to end throughout the earth.
He breaks the bow and snaps the spear;
he burns the shields with fire.

Oh, give me back my joy again;
you have broken me—
now let me rejoice.

All day long you plot destruction.
Your tongue cuts like a sharp razor;
you’re an expert at telling lies.

For strangers are attacking me;
violent people are trying to kill me.
They care nothing for God. Interlude

I will sacrifice a voluntary offering to you;
I will praise your name, O Lord,
for it is good.

Let death stalk my enemies;
let the grave swallow them alive,
for evil makes its home within them.

He ransoms me and keeps me safe
from the battle waged against me,
though many still oppose me.

My heart is confident in you, O God;
my heart is confident.
No wonder I can sing your praises!

Then at last everyone will say,
“There truly is a reward for those who live for God;
surely there is a God who judges justly here on earth.”

Listen to the filth that comes from their mouths;
their words cut like swords.
“After all, who can hear us?” they sneer.

Don’t kill them, for my people soon forget such lessons;
stagger them with your power, and bring them to their knees,
O Lord our shield.

O my Strength, to you I sing praises,
for you, O God, are my refuge,
the God who shows me unfailing love.

God has spoken plainly,
and I have heard it many times:
Power, O God, belongs to you;

O God, you are my God;
I earnestly search for you.
My soul thirsts for you;
my whole body longs for you
in this parched and weary land
where there is no water.

Then everyone will be afraid;
they will proclaim the mighty acts of God
and realize all the amazing things he does.

Everything on earth will worship you;
they will sing your praises,
shouting your name in glorious songs.” Interlude

For by his great power he rules forever.
He watches every movement of the nations;
let no rebel rise in defiance. Interlude

God places the lonely in families;
he sets the prisoners free and gives them joy.
But he makes the rebellious live in a sun-scorched land.

Tell everyone about God’s power.
His majesty shines down on Israel;
his strength is mighty in the heavens.

But as for me, I am poor and needy;
please hurry to my aid, O God.
You are my helper and my savior;
O Lord, do not delay.

Yes, you have been with me from birth;
from my mother’s womb you have cared for me.
No wonder I am always praising you!

My health may fail, and my spirit may grow weak,
but God remains the strength of my heart;
he is mine forever.

The clouds poured down rain;
the thunder rumbled in the sky.
Your arrows of lightning flashed.

Your thunder roared from the whirlwind;
the lightning lit up the world!
The earth trembled and shook.

He turned his anger against them;
he did not spare the Egyptians’ lives
but ravaged them with the plague.

He drove out the nations before them;
he gave them their inheritance by lot.
He settled the tribes of Israel into their homes.

You cried to me in trouble, and I saved you;
I answered out of the thundercloud
and tested your faith when there was no water at Meribah. Interlude

All the nations you made
will come and bow before you, Lord;
they will praise your holy name.

O God, insolent people rise up against me;
a violent gang is trying to kill me.
You mean nothing to them.

Look down and have mercy on me.
Give your strength to your servant;
save me, the son of your servant.

My eyes are blinded by my tears.
Each day I beg for your help, O Lord;
I lift my hands to you for mercy.

This I declare about the Lord:
He alone is my refuge, my place of safety;
he is my God, and I trust him.

When they call on me, I will answer;
I will be with them in trouble.
I will rescue and honor them.

The floods have risen up, O Lord.
The floods have roared like thunder;
the floods have lifted their pounding waves.

Enter his gates with thanksgiving;
go into his courts with praise.
Give thanks to him and praise his name.

you lay out the rafters of your home in the rain clouds.
You make the clouds your chariot;
you ride upon the wings of the wind.

Let me share in the prosperity of your chosen ones.
Let me rejoice in the joy of your people;
let me praise you with those who are your heritage.

Because of this, the earth opened up;
it swallowed Dathan
and buried Abiram and the other rebels.

For he has gathered the exiles from many lands,
from east and west,
from north and south.

Their ships were tossed to the heavens
and plunged again to the depths;
the sailors cringed in terror.

For he refused all kindness to others;
he persecuted the poor and needy,
and he hounded the brokenhearted to death.

He loved to curse others;
now you curse him.
He never blessed others;
now don’t you bless him.

He will punish the nations
and fill their lands with corpses;
he will shatter heads over the whole earth.

The wicked will see this and be infuriated.
They will grind their teeth in anger;
they will slink away, their hopes thwarted.

Death wrapped its ropes around me;
the terrors of the grave overtook me.
I saw only trouble and sorrow.

O Lord, I am your servant;
yes, I am your servant, born into your household;
you have freed me from my chains.

For his unfailing love for us is powerful;
the Lord’s faithfulness endures forever.
Praise the Lord!

They swarmed around me like bees;
they blazed against me like a crackling fire.
But I destroyed them all with the authority of the Lord.

Your laws please me;
they give me wise advice.
Daleth

O Lord, your unfailing love fills the earth;
teach me your decrees.
Teth

May I be blameless in keeping your decrees;
then I will never be ashamed.
Kaph

In your unfailing love, spare my life;
then I can continue to obey your laws.
Lamedh

Your commandments give me understanding;
no wonder I hate every false way of life.
Nun

I tremble in fear of you;
I stand in awe of your regulations.
Ayin

Your laws are always right;
help me to understand them so I may live.
Qoph

The very essence of your words is truth;
all your just regulations will stand forever.
Shin

I have wandered away like a lost sheep;
come and find me,
for I have not forgotten your commands.

It is useless for you to work so hard
from early morning until late at night,
anxiously working for food to eat;
for God gives rest to his loved ones.

I am counting on the Lord;
yes, I am counting on him.
I have put my hope in his word.

O Israel, hope in the Lord;
for with the Lord there is unfailing love.
His redemption overflows.

May my tongue stick to the roof of my mouth
if I fail to remember you,
if I don’t make Jerusalem my greatest joy.

The proud have set a trap to catch me;
they have stretched out a net;
they have placed traps all along the way. Interlude

Hear my prayer, O Lord;
listen to my plea!
Answer me because you are faithful and righteous.

Reach down from heaven and rescue me;
rescue me from deep waters,
from the power of my enemies.

He has not done this for any other nation;
they do not know his regulations.
Praise the Lord!

Let them all praise the name of the Lord.
For his name is very great;
his glory towers over the earth and heaven!

Praise the Lord!
Praise God in his sanctuary;
praise him in his mighty heaven!

The proverbs of Solomon:
A wise child brings joy to a father;
a foolish child brings grief to a mother.

The sayings of Agur son of Jakeh contain this message.
I am weary, O God;
I am weary and worn out, O God.

they chatter on and on.
No one really knows what is going to happen;
no one can predict the future.

How pleasing is your fragrance;
your name is like the spreading fragrance of scented oils.
No wonder all the young women love you!

You are so handsome, my love,
pleasing beyond words!
The soft grass is our bed;

Israel is full of silver and gold;
there is no end to its treasures.
Their land is full of warhorses;
there is no end to its chariots.

Their land is full of idols;
the people worship things they have made
with their own hands.

But this is what the Sovereign Lord says:
“This invasion will never happen;
it will never take place;

The Lord will consume Assyria’s glory
like a fire consumes a forest in a fruitful land;
it will waste away like sick people in a plague.

Nothing will hurt or destroy in all my holy mountain,
for as the waters fill the sea,
so the earth will be filled with people who know the Lord.

In that day the Lord will reach out his hand a second time
to bring back the remnant of his people—
those who remain in Assyria and northern Egypt;
in southern Egypt, Ethiopia, and Elam;
in Babylonia, Hamath, and all the distant coastlands.

What should we tell the Philistine messengers? Tell them,
“The Lord has built Jerusalem;
its walls will give refuge to his oppressed people.”

At that time the Lord of Heaven’s Armies will receive gifts
from this land divided by rivers,
from this tall, smooth-skinned people,
who are feared far and wide for their conquests and destruction.
They will bring the gifts to Jerusalem,
where the Lord of Heaven’s Armies dwells.

But now you are put to shame, city of Sidon,
for Tyre, the fortress of the sea, says,
“Now I am childless;
I have no sons or daughters.”

The cheerful sound of tambourines is stilled;
the happy cries of celebration are heard no more.
The melodious chords of the harp are silent.

The earth has broken up.
It has utterly collapsed;
it is violently shaken.

unless they turn to me for help.
Let them make peace with me;
yes, let them make peace with me.”

A heavy sledge is never used to thresh black cumin;
rather, it is beaten with a light stick.
A threshing wheel is never rolled on cumin;
instead, it is beaten lightly with a flail.

Join in the chorus, you desert towns;
let the villages of Kedar rejoice!
Let the people of Sela sing for joy;
shout praises from the mountaintops!

That is why I have disgraced your priests;
I have decreed complete destruction for Jacob
and shame for Israel.

This is what the Lord says—Israel’s King and Redeemer, the Lord of Heaven’s Armies:
“I am the First and the Last;
there is no other God.

He cuts down cedars;
he selects the cypress and the oak;
he plants the pine in the forest
to be nourished by the rain.

But they are like straw burning in a fire;
they cannot save themselves from the flame.
You will get no help from them at all;
their hearth is no place to sit for warmth.

See, my people will return from far away,
from lands to the north and west,
and from as far south as Egypt.”

“Look and see, for everyone is coming home!
Your sons are coming from distant lands;
your little daughters will be carried home.

O Jerusalem, I have posted watchmen on your walls;
they will pray day and night, continually.
Take no rest, all you who pray to the Lord.

Your holy cities are destroyed.
Zion is a wilderness;
yes, Jerusalem is a desolate ruin.

The plain of Sharon will again be filled with flocks
for my people who have searched for me,
and the valley of Achor will be a place to pasture herds.

They will devour the food of your harvest;
they will devour your sons and daughters.
They will devour your flocks and herds;
they will devour your grapes and figs.
And they will destroy your fortified towns,
which you think are so safe.

They all fool and defraud each other;
no one tells the truth.
With practiced tongues they tell lies;
they wear themselves out with all their sinning.

I will weep for the mountains
and wail for the wilderness pastures.
For they are desolate and empty of life;
the lowing of cattle is heard no more;
the birds and wild animals have all fled.

Listen, you women, to the words of the Lord;
open your ears to what he has to say.
Teach your daughters to wail;
teach one another how to lament.

And if they say to you, ‘But where can we go?’ tell them, ‘This is what the Lord says:
“‘Those who are destined for death, to death;
those who are destined for war, to war;
those who are destined for famine, to famine;
those who are destined for captivity, to captivity.’

O Lord, if you heal me, I will be truly healed;
if you save me, I will be truly saved.
My praises are for you alone!

This is what the Lord says:
“I hear cries of fear;
there is terror and no peace.

There will be joy and songs of thanksgiving,
and I will multiply my people, not diminish them;
I will honor them, not despise them.

Set up road signs;
put up guideposts.
Mark well the path
by which you came.
Come back again, my virgin Israel;
return to your towns here.

Egypt flees, silent as a serpent gliding away.
The invading army marches in;
they come against her with axes like woodsmen.

This message was given concerning Moab. This is what the Lord of Heaven’s Armies, the God of Israel, says:
“What sorrow awaits the city of Nebo;
it will soon lie in ruins.
The city of Kiriathaim will be humiliated and captured;
the fortress will be humiliated and broken down.

So now I wail for Moab;
yes, I will mourn for Moab.
My heart is broken for the men of Kir-hareseth.

Her cities now lie in ruins;
she is a dry wasteland
where no one lives or even passes by.

“For all these things I weep;
tears flow down my cheeks.
No one is here to comfort me;
any who might encourage me are far away.
My children have no future,
for the enemy has conquered us.”

O beautiful Jerusalem, your punishment will end;
you will soon return from exile.
But Edom, your punishment is just beginning;
soon your many sins will be exposed.

Drunkenness and anguish will fill you,
for your cup is filled to the brim with distress and desolation,
the same cup your sister Samaria drank.

But it’s hopeless;
the corruption can’t be cleaned out.
So throw it into the fire.

“Son of man, prophesy and give this message from the Sovereign Lord:
“Weep and wail
for that day,

Yes, I will set fire to all Egypt!
Pelusium will be racked with pain;
Thebes will be torn apart;
Memphis will live in constant terror.

“The arrogance of Israel testifies against her;
Israel and Ephraim will stumble under their load of guilt.
Judah, too, will fall with them.

“Come, let us return to the Lord.
He has torn us to pieces;
now he will heal us.
He has injured us;
now he will bandage our wounds.

The people of Israel feed on the wind;
they chase after the east wind all day long.
They pile up lies and violence;
they are making an alliance with Assyria
while sending olive oil to buy support from Egypt.

I will gather the armies of the world
into the valley of Jehoshaphat.
There I will judge them
for harming my people, my special possession,
for scattering my people among the nations,
and for dividing up my land.

You will be led out through the ruins of the wall;
you will be thrown from your fortresses,”
says the Lord.

The Lord says to Edom,
“I will cut you down to size among the nations;
you will be greatly despised.

You rulers make decisions based on bribes;
you priests teach God’s laws only for a price;
you prophets won’t prophesy unless you are paid.
Yet all of you claim to depend on the Lord.
“No harm can come to us,” you say,
“for the Lord is here among us.”

People from many lands will come and honor you—
from Assyria all the way to the towns of Egypt,
from Egypt all the way to the Euphrates River,
and from distant seas and mountains.

Why are you scheming against the Lord?
He will destroy you with one blow;
he won’t need to strike twice!

The king shouts to his officers;
they stumble in their haste,
rushing to the walls to set up their defenses.

Even though the fig trees have no blossoms,
and there are no grapes on the vines;
even though the olive crop fails,
and the fields lie empty and barren;
even though the flocks die in the fields,
and the cattle barns are empty,

‘Father will be divided against son
and son against father;
mother against daughter
and daughter against mother;
and mother-in-law against daughter-in-law
and daughter-in-law against mother-in-law.’”

In fact, it says,
“The message is very close at hand;
it is on your lips and in your heart.” And that message is the very message about faith that we preach:

He also came so that the Gentiles might give glory to God for his mercies to them. That is what the psalmist meant when he wrote:
“For this, I will praise you among the Gentiles;
I will sing praises to your name.”

And again,
“The Lord knows the thoughts of the wise;
he knows they are worthless.”

“Once you had no identity as a people;
now you are God’s people.
Once you received no mercy;
now you have received God’s mercy.”
